/-!
# The canonical map `(Rf_*K)^∧ ⟶ Rf_*(K^∧)` is invertible

[Stacks, Tag 0A0G] says that `Rf_*` commutes with derived completion: for a morphism of ringed
sites `f : (Sh(𝒟), 𝒪') ⟶ (Sh(𝒞), 𝒪)`, a sheaf of ideals `ℐ ⊂ 𝒪` and `ℐ' ⊂ 𝒪'` the ideal
generated by `f^♯(f^{-1}ℐ)`, the canonical map
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison`,

`(Rf_*K)^∧ ⟶ Rf_*(K^∧)`,

is invertible for every object `K` of `D(𝒪')`. This file proves exactly that — invertibility of
the canonical map, for an arbitrary `K` — at `ℐ = (t_1, …, t_r)` and `ℐ' = (f^♯t_1, …, f^♯t_r)`
generated by finitely many global sections of the two structure sheaves, the regime the source's
own proof reduces to; and then at the source's own `ℐ' = f^{-1}ℐ · 𝒪_X`, built from `ℐ` rather
than given.

## From the display to the canonical map

`RingedSite.nonempty_iso_derivedPushforward_derivedCompletion_generatedIdealSheaf`
of `PushforwardCommutesCompletionHomTower.lean` runs the source's argument and produces the
display as the source writes it, `Nonempty (Rf_*(K^∧) ≅ (Rf_*K)^∧)`: the two sides are derived
limits of isomorphic towers, and uniqueness of derived limits ([Stacks, Tag 0H9L]) is not
canonical, so the isomorphism it produces is not the canonical map.

The gap between the two closes by applying the display **at the cone of the completion map**
rather than at `K`. Write `L' = (-)^∧` on `D(𝒪')` and `L = (-)^∧` on `D(𝒪)`, and let `C` sit in a
distinguished triangle `K ⟶ L'K ⟶ C ⟶ K⟦1⟧`.

* `C` admits no nonzero map to a derived-complete object, by the universal property of `L'`
  alone: a map `C ⟶ Z` dies on `L'K`, hence factors through `K⟦1⟧`, hence through `(L'K)⟦1⟧`
  because the derived-complete objects are stable under the shift, and the composite of two
  consecutive maps of a distinguished triangle vanishes. This is
  `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.leftOrthogonal_isDerivedComplete_cone_derivedCompletionUnit`,
  and the shift-stability it rests on is
  `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isDerivedComplete_shiftFunctor_obj` of `CompleteShift.lean`.
* Admitting no nonzero map to a derived-complete object is the same as having vanishing derived
  completion (`leftOrthogonal_isDerivedComplete_iff_isZero_derivedCompletion`), so `L'C = 0`;
  the display **at `C`** then gives `L(Rf_*C) ≅ Rf_*(L'C) = 0`, that is, `Rf_*C` admits no
  nonzero map to a derived-complete object either, and neither does `(Rf_*C)⟦-1⟧`.
* `Rf_*` is exact, so `Rf_*K ⟶ Rf_*(L'K) ⟶ Rf_*C` is a distinguished triangle; a long exact
  sequence with both outer terms orthogonal to `N` makes composition with `Rf_*(K ⟶ K^∧)` a
  bijection `Hom(Rf_*(K^∧), N) ≃ Hom(Rf_*K, N)` for every derived-complete `N`. That is the
  criterion
  `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_iff_bijective`
  of `PushforwardCompletionUniversalProperty.lean`, and it says precisely that the canonical map
  is invertible.

So nothing of the source's computation is redone: the display at the level of objects, quantified
over **all** objects, already implies that the canonical map is invertible. That implication is
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_of_nonempty_iso`, and
it holds at an arbitrary pair of sheaves of ideals.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_of_nonempty_iso`:
  the canonical map is invertible at every object as soon as the display holds at every object,
  for an arbitrary pair of sheaves of ideals related by `f^♯`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_generatedIdealSheaf`
  and
  `RingedSite.isIso_natTrans_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_generatedIdealSheaf`:
  **[Stacks, Tag 0A0G] for the canonical map and an arbitrary object**, at
  `ℐ = (t_1, …, t_r) ⊂ 𝒪` and `ℐ' = (f^♯t_1, …, f^♯t_r) ⊂ 𝒪'`, objectwise and as an
  isomorphism of functors.
* `RingedSite.isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_inverseImageIdealSheaf`
  and
  `RingedSite.isIso_natTrans_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_inverseImageIdealSheaf`:
  the same at the source's own `ℐ' = f^{-1}ℐ · 𝒪_X`, which
  `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.eq_generatedIdealSheaf_pushGlobalSections` identifies with the
  ideal sheaf generated by `f^♯t_1, …, f^♯t_r`.

## What the statements ask of the data

Only properties of the data, never of the object `K`, which ranges over all of `D(𝒪')`:

* `ℐ` is generated by finitely many global sections `t_1, …, t_r` of `𝒪`. This is the regime the
  source's proof reduces to by a localization, and it is the regime in which [Stacks, Tag 099F]
  is available here, so that both derived completion functors are produced from the generators
  and no conclusion of that tag is an input. The source's `ℐ` is of finite type, hence only
  *locally* generated by finitely many sections, and [Stacks, Tag 099F] at such an `ℐ` rests on
  [Stacks, Tags 099D and 099E]; the module documentation of
  `PushforwardCommutesCompletionGenerated.lean` records what stands in the way of the
  local-to-global descent.
* `ℐ'` is generated by `f^♯t_1, …, f^♯t_r`
  (`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.PushGlobalSectionsCompatible`), which is the source's own
  relation between the two ideal sheaves; in the second pair of statements it is discharged from
  `f` and `t`, the transported family being
  `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.pushGlobalSections`, available as soon as the target site has a
  terminal object whose image is terminal — as at ringed spaces and at schemes.

## Implementation notes

*Where the shift enters.* Everything in the `Orthogonal` section is the statement that the
objects admitting no nonzero map to a derived-complete object — Mathlib's
`CategoryTheory.ObjectProperty.leftOrthogonal` of the derived-complete objects — behave like a
triangulated subcategory. Mathlib supplies that from
`CategoryTheory.ObjectProperty.IsStableUnderShift`, which is why `CompleteShift.lean` exists:
without shift-stability of derived completeness, a map out of the cone could be factored through
`K⟦1⟧` and no further.

*Why the engine takes the display at every object.* It consumes the display at the cone `C`,
which is built from `K` by a non-constructive choice, so quantifying over all objects is the
honest shape of the hypothesis; at the globally generated ideal sheaves it is available for all
objects anyway.

*The exactness of `Rf_*`.* The `CategoryTheory.Functor.CommShift` and
`CategoryTheory.Functor.IsTriangulated` structures of `Rf_*` are produced inside the proof, as
they are in
`Stacks.Cohomology.DerivedPushforwardOnRingedTopoi.isDerivedLimit_map_derivedPushforward`:
`CategoryTheory.Functor.IsRightDerivedFunctor.commShift` and
`Stacks.Injectives.rightDerivedFunctor_isTriangulated`, the latter because sheaves of modules on
a site form a Grothendieck abelian category.

